Leakage-Aware Evaluation and Benchmarks for Common Skin Disease
Classification Using the Bangladesh Clinical Image Dataset

Hasan Imam Bappi1, Mirza Hamid Al Wasi2, Alvir Shahriar3
1,2,3 Department of Computer Science and Engineering, International Islamic University
Chittagong (IIUC), Chattogram, Bangladesh

Abstract

Clinical skin-image datasets released as pre-augmented folders can leak information across
training and test partitions: derivatives of one raw photograph may appear in both. We audit the
Bangladesh Common Skin Disease Image Dataset and quantify this effect under a source-image-disjoint
protocol. The public release contains 197 raw images, of which 136 have a recoverable class, and
3,224 augmented images (1,612 at 176x176 and 1,612 at 512x512). We reconstruct source groups with
a conservative over-merged grouping and evaluate only raw images in validation and test sets.
Frozen DINOv2 and ResNet-50 features with 1-nearest-neighbour and linear probes score 0.54-0.66
macro-F1 under source-image-disjoint evaluation, but reach approximately 1.00 under image-level
splitting. A paired dose-response experiment over all five folds and three repeats shows that
partial leakage raises macro-F1 by 0.250-0.361, with monotone curves and 95% confidence intervals
excluding zero. Shortcut probes find a weak acquisition signal (metadata-only macro-F1 0.343)
but no global colour shortcut (0.246 versus 0.230 for a stratified dummy). Automated label triage
flags 23 of 136 raw images from frozen probes and 16 from a stronger fine-tuned model, dominated
by minority classes predicted as Eczema and therefore inconclusive. Fine-tuned ResNet-50 pilots
reach 0.604 macro-F1 at 128 px (one repeat) and 0.681 [0.546,0.799] at 160 px with class-balanced
sampling over five folds and three repeats; the latter is not clearly above the frozen-feature
floor. These results show that headline accuracy on the released augmented set is not a reliable
estimate of clinical screening performance. The final benchmark, calibration analysis and clinical
validation remain future work.

1. Introduction

1.1 Background

Skin disease is common in Bangladesh, whereas specialist dermatologists are scarce. A classifier
that works on smartphone photographs could support referral decisions in primary care,
tele-dermatology and public-health surveillance. Deep learning has made substantial progress on
dermatoscopic image classification, supported by public benchmarks such as HAM10000 [1],
multi-scale EfficientNet ensembles [2], transformer models [3] and imbalance-aware losses [4].
However, dermoscopy and clinical photography differ in acquisition conditions, field of view and
image quality. Results on dermoscopic benchmarks do not transfer directly to routine clinical
photographs.

Two Bangladeshi clinical image datasets have recently been released. The Bangladesh Common Skin
Disease Image Dataset [5] contains five conditions: contact dermatitis, eczema, scabies, tinea
(ringworm) and vitiligo. SkinDisNet [6] provides patient-level metadata and overlaps four of these
classes. Both datasets organise images into original and augmented folders. This organisation is
useful for training but dangerous for evaluation: a random split over augmented files can place
derivatives of the same photograph in both training and test sets.

1.2 Problem

The released version of the dataset [5] reports 1,612 augmented images and a small number of raw
photographs. Random image-level splitting therefore risks measuring memorisation of augmented
copies rather than generalisation to a new clinical image. The original benchmark reports high
accuracy but does not address this source-level leakage. It also does not report modern pretrained
baselines, per-class screening metrics or uncertainty estimates. As a result, the apparent
performance of automated skin-disease classification on this dataset is difficult to interpret.

This paper makes four contributions. First, we audit the dataset and reconstruct source groups
without relying on author-provided patient identifiers. Second, we design three evaluation
protocols: a reproduced image-level split, a source-image-disjoint split and a controlled
partial-leakage split. Third, we quantify leakage with paired dose-response experiments using
frozen features, group bootstrap intervals and permutation tests. Fourth, we test whether simple
acquisition shortcuts or label-quality problems can explain the observed performance floor.
We also report fine-tuned ResNet-50 pilots to validate the training pipeline; they are not the
final model benchmark.

1.3 Research questions

RQ1. How much does image-level splitting of pre-augmented data inflate performance?
RQ2. Which source-image-disjoint performance floor is obtained with frozen visual features?
RQ3. Are simple acquisition or colour cues sufficient to explain that floor?
RQ4. Do automated label flags provide credible evidence of label noise?

2. Related Work

Tschandl et al. [1] introduced HAM10000, a multi-source dermoscopic benchmark that enabled
human-versus-machine comparison. Gessert et al. [2] combined multi-resolution EfficientNets with
metadata and won the ISIC 2019 challenge, showing the value of pretrained convolutional
ensembles. Ayas [3] applied a Swin Transformer to dermoscopic classification; the gap between
accuracy and balanced accuracy in that work illustrates why overall accuracy is inadequate for
imbalanced medical data. Li et al. [4] proposed the SCTFD framework with oversampling and a
Focal+Dice loss for imbalanced dermoscopic data, but evaluated it only on dermoscopy.

Islam et al. [5] released the Bangladeshi clinical image dataset considered here and benchmarked
classical machine-learning and deep-learning models. The study reported high accuracy on the
augmented images, but subject-level or source-image-level separation was not addressed. Sultana
et al. [6] released SkinDisNet with patient-level metadata; its augmented folders can also leak
unless derivatives are grouped by original photograph. Neither study provides a leakage-controlled
benchmark with per-class screening metrics on the target dataset.

Leakage from data augmentation is a general problem in medical imaging. If the evaluation unit is
an augmented file rather than an independent source image, the effective sample size is inflated
and confidence intervals are too narrow. The correct unit here is the raw image. Because the
dataset has no patient identifiers, we use the term source-image-disjoint rather than
subject-disjoint: the same patient may have contributed multiple raw photographs. Descriptive
comparisons between image-level and source-disjoint protocols are unpaired because they evaluate
different images. The rigorous leakage estimate is therefore a paired partial-leakage contrast on
the same raw test images.

3. Dataset and Audit

3.1 Released data

The Mendeley release v3 (DOI: 10.17632/9ggd3shdr7.3) contains three folders:

Folder                 Content                                      Count
ResearchImage/         raw photographs                              197
Images_v1/             augmented images, 176x176                    1,612
Images_v2/             augmented images, 512x512                    1,612

The raw files are numbered 1.jpg to 198.jpg with 169.jpg missing. The paper's approximate count
of 250 is a collection claim, not the number present in the public release. v1 and v2 are
independent augmentation passes rather than duplicate resolutions of the same files. All 3,224
augmented images must therefore be considered when reconstructing source groups.

No exact duplicates were found by SHA-256 or MD5. Perceptual hashing found 451 near-duplicate
clusters covering 1,251 files. Filename collisions and derivative overlap make a naive split
unsafe.

3.2 Labelled images and source groups

Only 136 of the 197 raw images have a recoverable class. The other 61 are unlabelled and are
excluded from all supervised experiments. Raw labels are inferred from the folder labels of their
augmented descendants and agree with descendant labels in 98.9% of group-level checks; they are
not author-provided annotations.

Source groups were reconstructed in three stages: filename/dihedral matching of raw images to
derivatives, SIFT/RANSAC geometric confirmation, and embedding-based assignment of remaining
images. Tier-A groups have high anchor agreement and are used for training; tier-B assignments are
conservative but less reliable. The resulting 135 raw super-groups are deliberately over-merged,
so they are stricter than a hypothetical ground-truth grouping but cannot support exact claims
about the number of patients or lesions. The labelled raw class distribution is imbalanced:
Eczema 73, Tinea Ringworm 29, Dermatitis 13, Vitiligo 11 and Scabies 10.

