4. Methods

4.1 Evaluation unit and protocols

The primary evaluation unit is the raw image. Validation and test partitions contain raw images
only. Training partitions may contain raw images and tier-A derivatives from training groups.
This prevents pseudo-replication: several derivatives of one raw test image are not counted as
independent test cases.

P-leaked reproduces the original image-level protocol: a stratified 70/15/15 split of the 1,612
v1 images, repeated three times. Derivatives of one source may cross roles. This protocol measures
memorisation under the published evaluation style, but its test set differs from the other
protocols and is reported descriptively.

P-disjoint is the primary protocol. It uses repeated StratifiedGroupKFold with five folds and
three repeats on the 136 labelled raw images, grouped by reconstructed source group. Test and
validation contain raw images only; training contains raw and tier-A derivative images from
training groups. Automated checks assert that no group crosses partitions, no near-duplicate pair
crosses partitions and all classes are present in training and test.

P-partial starts from every P-disjoint fold and repeat and adds derivatives of 0%, 25%, 50% or
100% of test groups to the training set. The raw test images remain unchanged. This yields a
paired dose-response experiment: the same raw images are evaluated at each leakage level.

Table 2. Evaluation protocols.

Protocol   Construction                                             Role
P-leaked   image-level 70/15/15 over v1 derivatives, 3 repeats      reproduction, unpaired
P-disjoint 5-fold x 3-repeat group CV, raw-only test                 primary endpoint
P-partial  derivatives of 0/25/50/100% of test groups added to train paired leakage effect

4.2 Features and probes

For frozen-feature experiments, images are resized to 224x224. Two ImageNet-pretrained feature
extractors are used without fine-tuning: ResNet-50 [8] and DINOv2 ViT-S/14 [18]. Two probes are
applied to each feature family: 1-nearest-neighbour with cosine distance, and multinomial logistic
regression with C=1.0, balanced class weights and a fixed maximum of 2,000 iterations. These
probes are deliberately simple: they act as leakage detectors rather than final classifiers.

The fine-tuning pilots use timm's resnet50.a1_in1k, AdamW with learning rate 3e-4 and weight decay
0.05, cross-entropy with label smoothing 0.1, batch size 32, random resized crop, horizontal flip,
rotation and colour jitter. The first uses 128x128 input, at most 10 epochs and only repeat 0 of
the five P-disjoint folds. The second uses 160x160 input, a cosine schedule, class-balanced
sampling, all five folds and three repeats with seed 42, and at most 12 epochs. Both use early
stopping with patience 3 on validation macro-F1 and evaluate raw images only.

4.3 Shortcut and label audits

Three shortcut families were tested on the same 5x3 P-disjoint folds. Metadata probes use raw
image width, height, aspect ratio, file size, bytes per pixel, JPEG quality and available EXIF
fields. Colour probes use mean and standard deviation of RGB and HSV channels plus 8-bin
histograms. Masked-image probes re-embed four variants: full image, border20 (outer band only),
background50 (central 50% masked) and background75 (central 75% masked). Because no lesion masks
exist, background variants are explicit proxies, not true background segmentation.

Label quality was triaged with cleanlab confident learning on pooled out-of-fold probabilities
from the four frozen probes. An image is a high-confidence issue when at least three of four
probes flag it. This is a review shortlist, not evidence that a label is wrong.

4.4 Metrics and statistics

The primary endpoint is macro-F1 on pooled out-of-fold raw predictions. Secondary metrics are
balanced accuracy, accuracy, per-class recall and confusion patterns. Intervals are group
bootstrap intervals over reconstructed group IDs with 1,000 resamples. The leakage effect is the
paired difference Delta = macro-F1(P-partial 100%) - macro-F1(P-partial 0%) on the same raw test
images, with the same group resamples used for both levels. A group-level permutation test with
1,000 permutations provides a two-sided p-value. Holm correction is specified for families of
model comparisons. McNemar's test is not used for P-leaked versus P-disjoint because those
protocols have different test sets.

5. Results

5.1 Dataset and split validation

The audit produced 3,421 image files and verified source-disjointness across all 15 P-disjoint
triplets and all 60 P-partial triplets. Achieved leakage levels for requested 0/25/50/100% were
0.0/22.9/50.0/100.0%. Per-fold raw test sets contain 27-28 images from 17-19 groups. This small
size limits class-specific claims, especially for Scabies and Dermatitis.

