Full-image frozen probes score 0.627-0.669. Border20 drops them to 0.467-0.538. The mild
background50 mask drops them only to 0.537-0.621, while the stricter background75 mask drops them
to 0.401-0.532. Masked variants remain above the majority baseline, but the strict mask approaches
the stratified dummy. These results do not support a strong global colour shortcut. They indicate
a weak acquisition cue and residual border/crop signal that cannot be separated from disease
signal without lesion masks.

5.4 Label-quality triage

Per-probe cleanlab flags ranged from 28 to 50 raw images. In consensus, 23 of 136 images (16.9%)
were flagged by at least three probes and 35 (25.7%) by at least two. Flag rates were highest for
Scabies (70%), Dermatitis (54%) and Tinea Ringworm (24%), and lowest for Vitiligo (9%) and Eczema
(1.4%). Among the 23 high-confidence flags, 18 were predicted as Eczema by the consensus model.

This pattern mirrors model confusion rather than independent evidence of mislabelling: Eczema is
the majority class, and minority-class recall is low. The audit is useful for prioritising
clinician review, but no images were removed or relabelled.

