6. Discussion

The main finding is that source-level leakage changes the apparent difficulty of this dataset by
25-36 macro-F1 points. A near-perfect score under image-level splitting is not evidence of a
strong clinical classifier: a 1-NN probe on frozen features reaches the same result when
derivatives of a test source are available in training. The source-disjoint floor of 0.54-0.66 is
more plausible, and even that floor may be optimistic because groups are reconstructed rather
than supplied and may still contain repeated patients.

The dose-response curve is more informative than a single before/after comparison. The jump
between 25% and 50% leakage suggests that memorisation emerges once a substantial fraction of test
sources has derivatives in training. The paired design avoids the invalid comparison of different
test sets and gives a direct estimate of the inflation on the same raw images.

Shortcut probes partially explain why the disjoint floor is not lower. Acquisition metadata alone
is above chance, and border-only inputs retain 0.47-0.54 macro-F1, so the model may exploit
crop, background or acquisition cues. However, colour features are at chance and strict masks
reduce performance substantially. The appropriate interpretation is that a weak acquisition
shortcut exists, not that the model has learned only shortcuts. A clinician-marked lesion mask
subset would be needed to separate these possibilities.

The label audit also warrants caution. A 17% high-confidence flag rate is implausibly high for a
dataset whose descendant labels agree almost perfectly. Because most flags are minority images
predicted as Eczema, they probably reflect model bias toward the majority class. They should be
reviewed by a clinician, but they should not be used to delete data. Repeating the audit after a
stronger class-balanced model flags 16 images, 14 of them the same images, so the shortlist is
stable but still inconclusive.

The 128 px pilot did not challenge the frozen-feature floor, which was expected given its low
resolution, short schedule and single repeat. The stronger 160 px, class-balanced, 5x3 reference
reaches 0.681 macro-F1 and closes that gap without a statistically clear win over DINOv2. Only one
seed was affordable on CPU, so seed variance is not yet measured, and Scabies recall remains low.
The next step is a shortlist of models with additional seeds, followed by training-trick ablations.
Calibration, triage thresholds, robustness testing and external validation on SkinDisNet are
required before any deployment claim can be considered.

7. Limitations

This is a secondary analysis of a public dataset. No patient identifiers are available, so the
protocol is source-image-disjoint, not patient-disjoint. Source groups are reconstructive and
over-merged; they are conservative for leakage control but reduce statistical power. Only 136 of
197 raw images have recoverable labels, and those labels are inferred from descendants rather than
provided by clinicians. Per-class test support is small, especially for Scabies and Dermatitis.

P-leaked and P-disjoint evaluate different image sets and are not paired; the paired P-partial
Delta is the defensible leakage estimate. Shortcut masks are crude proxies because no lesion
segmentation exists. Metadata coverage is limited. Label triage is model-dependent and awaits
clinical review. The fine-tuned results are pilots, not a final benchmark, and only one seed was
run. Reference metadata must be verified before submission, and clinician review, ethics
confirmation, licence confirmation and journal selection remain outstanding.

8. Conclusion

Image-level splitting of pre-augmented clinical skin images produces a severe and quantifiable
optimism bias. Under a source-image-disjoint protocol, frozen visual features reach 0.54-0.66
macro-F1, while partial leakage raises performance by 25-36 points. Metadata provides a weak
shortcut, global colour does not, and automated label flags are not yet credible evidence of
mislabelling. Fine-tuned ResNet-50 pilots reach 0.604 macro-F1 at 128 px and 0.681 at 160 px with
class-balanced sampling; neither clearly beats the frozen-feature floor. The released splits,
audit artifacts and scripts provide a reproducible basis for the remaining benchmark, calibration,
external-validation and clinical review work.

